### What is the DAO pattern?

The DAO (Data Access Object) pattern abstracts access to a specific data source. A DAO typically mirrors a table and exposes storage-oriented operations like insert, findById, update, and delete. It is close to the storage mechanism and often contains SQL or JPA calls directly. You might have one DAO per table and compose them in the service layer.

```java
package com.bank.dao;

import com.bank.model.Account;
import jakarta.persistence.EntityManager;
import jakarta.persistence.PersistenceContext;
import java.util.Optional;

public class AccountDao {

    @PersistenceContext
    private EntityManager em;

    public void insert(Account account) {
        em.persist(account);
    }

    public Optional<Account> findById(String accountId) {
        Account account = em.find(Account.class, accountId);
        return Optional.ofNullable(account);
    }

    public void update(Account account) {
        em.merge(account);
    }

    public void delete(String accountId) {
        Account account = em.find(Account.class, accountId);
        if (account != null) {
            em.remove(account);
        }
    }
}
```

```text
DAO characteristics:
- Storage-oriented: insert, update, delete, findById
- One DAO per table or entity
- Contains JPA/JDBC calls directly
- Developer writes all the implementation code
```

### What is the Repository pattern?

The Repository pattern, from Domain-Driven Design, is a higher-level abstraction that presents a collection-like interface for aggregate roots. Instead of exposing raw CRUD, it expresses domain intent through methods like findOverdrawnAccounts or findByHolderPan. In Spring Data, extending JpaRepository gives you a Repository whose implementation is generated at runtime.

```java
package com.bank.repository;

import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.JpaRepository;
import com.bank.model.Account;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;

public interface AccountRepository extends JpaRepository<Account, String> {

    // Domain-intent queries: no implementation needed
    List<Account> findByBalanceLessThan(BigDecimal threshold);

    List<Account> findByHolderPan(String pan);

    List<Account> findByStatusAndBranchCode(String status, String branchCode);
}
```

```text
Repository characteristics:
- Domain-oriented: findOverdrawnAccounts, findByHolderPan
- Interface-based, Spring generates implementation at runtime
- No boilerplate code to write
- Speaks the language of the business, not the storage
```

### How do DAO and Repository differ?

| Aspect | DAO | Repository |
|---|---|---|
| Origin | Core J2EE pattern | Domain-Driven Design |
| Abstraction level | Table/storage-oriented | Domain/aggregate-oriented |
| Implementation | Developer writes it manually | Spring Data generates it at runtime |
| Method naming | insert, update, delete | save, findByX (domain intent) |
| Granularity | One per table | One per aggregate root |
| Boilerplate | Significant | Minimal (just an interface) |
| Domain contract | No aggregate guarantees | Manages aggregate roots as a unit |

```java
// DAO: storage-oriented, manual implementation
package com.bank.dao;

public class AccountDao {
    public void insert(Account account) { em.persist(account); }
    public void update(Account account) { em.merge(account); }
    public void delete(String id)       { em.remove(em.find(Account.class, id)); }
}

// Repository: domain-oriented, auto-generated
package com.bank.repository;

import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.JpaRepository;
import com.bank.model.Account;

public interface AccountRepository extends JpaRepository<Account, String> {
    // Spring generates all CRUD + findByX + paging + batch methods
}
```

### How do they enable a swappable data store?

Because callers depend on an interface, you can provide a JPA implementation today and a MongoDB or in-memory implementation tomorrow without touching the service. The key is not leaking store-specific types across the boundary.

```java
// Service depends on the interface, not the implementation
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.repository.AccountRepository;
import com.bank.model.Account;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;

public class OverdraftDetectionService {

    private final AccountRepository accountRepository;

    public OverdraftDetectionService(AccountRepository accountRepository) {
        this.accountRepository = accountRepository;
    }

    public List<Account> findOverdrawnAccounts() {
        return accountRepository.findByBalanceLessThan(BigDecimal.ZERO);
    }
}
```

```text
Swappable store demonstration:
- Production: Spring Data JPA provides the AccountRepository implementation (PostgreSQL)
- Integration test: H2 in-memory database backs the same interface
- Unit test: a mock or in-memory stub backs the same interface
- Future: MongoDB implementation could replace JPA without changing OverdraftDetectionService
```

### How do they enable testability?

Both patterns let you mock or substitute the data-access interface in unit tests. With a Repository, you can mock it directly because it is an interface. With a DAO, you can depend on an interface and provide a test stub.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import org.mockito.Mockito;
import com.bank.repository.AccountRepository;
import com.bank.model.Account;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*;
import static org.mockito.Mockito.*;

class OverdraftDetectionServiceTest {

    @Test
    void findsOverdrawnAccounts() {
        AccountRepository mockRepo = mock(AccountRepository.class);
        Account overdrawn = new Account("SAV2001", "Rajesh", new BigDecimal("-500.00"));
        when(mockRepo.findByBalanceLessThan(BigDecimal.ZERO))
                .thenReturn(List.of(overdrawn));

        OverdraftDetectionService service = new OverdraftDetectionService(mockRepo);
        List<Account> result = service.findOverdrawnAccounts();

        assertEquals(1, result.size());
        assertEquals("SAV2001", result.get(0).getAccountId());
    }
}
```

```text
Expected test output:
- Test passes with no database connection
- Business logic tested in isolation
- Mock returns predefined data
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is leaking store-specific types across the repository boundary. If the repository returns JPA entities with lazy proxies, the abstraction is not truly swappable because the caller depends on JPA behaviour. The fix is to return domain objects or DTOs from the repository boundary.

```java
// WRONG: leaking JPA entity with lazy proxy across the boundary
package com.bank.controller;

import com.bank.repository.AccountRepository;
import com.bank.model.Account;

public class AccountController {
    public Account getAccount(String id) {
        Account account = accountRepository.findById(id).orElseThrow();
        // account.getTrades() might be a Hibernate lazy proxy
        // If OSIV is off, this throws LazyInitializationException
        // The "swappable store" abstraction is broken
        return account;
    }
}
```

```java
// CORRECT: return a DTO from the service layer
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.repository.AccountRepository;
import com.bank.dto.AccountDto;

public class AccountService {
    private final AccountRepository accountRepository;

    public AccountService(AccountRepository accountRepository) {
        this.accountRepository = accountRepository;
    }

    public AccountDto getAccount(String id) {
        return accountRepository.findById(id)
                .map(a -> new AccountDto(a.getAccountId(), a.getHolderName(), a.getBalance()))
                .orElseThrow();
        // DTO is a plain object, no lazy proxy, truly swappable
    }
}
```

### When should you use Repository vs DAO?

Use Spring Data Repository for the common case because it eliminates boilerplate and expresses domain intent. Use a hand-written DAO or a repository custom-implementation fragment when you need fine-grained control over SQL, stored procedures, or a non-JPA data source.

```java
// Custom fragment: combine Repository convenience with DAO control
package com.bank.repository;

import com.bank.model.Account;
import java.util.List;

public interface AccountRepositoryCustom {
    List<Account> findByComplexCriteria(String region, String status, int minAge);
}
```

```java
package com.bank.repository;

import com.bank.model.Account;
import jakarta.persistence.EntityManager;
import jakarta.persistence.PersistenceContext;
import java.util.List;

public class AccountRepositoryCustomImpl implements AccountRepositoryCustom {

    @PersistenceContext
    private EntityManager em;

    @Override
    public List<Account> findByComplexCriteria(String region, String status, int minAge) {
        // Hand-written JPA criteria or native SQL when Spring Data cannot express it
        return em.createQuery(
                "SELECT a FROM Account a WHERE a.region = :region AND a.status = :status",
                Account.class)
                .setParameter("region", region)
                .setParameter("status", status)
                .getResultList();
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer knows that in strict DDD, a Repository manages whole aggregates, loading and saving an aggregate root as a unit to protect its invariants. A DAO does not carry that domain contract. They also know that hiding the store behind an interface is only truly swappable if you avoid leaking store-specific types. They use Spring Data repositories for the common case and drop to a custom implementation fragment for complex queries.

```java
package com.bank.repository;

import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.JpaRepository;
import com.bank.model.Account;

// Combine auto-generated methods with custom fragment
public interface AccountRepository
        extends JpaRepository<Account, String>, AccountRepositoryCustom {
    // Spring Data generates CRUD + paging methods
    // AccountRepositoryCustomImpl provides the complex query
}
```
